        "text": "4"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "23"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Three terms work, two do not",
        "body": "Three terms suffice: $\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac1{60}=\\frac{30+15+1}{60}=\\frac{46}{60}=\\frac{23}{30}$. Suppose two terms worked: $\\frac1p+\\frac1q=\\frac{23}{30}$ with $p\\ge q$. Then $\\frac2q\\ge\\frac{23}{30}$, so $q\\le\\frac{60}{23}<3$, so $q=2$ (since $q=1$ gives $1>\\frac{23}{30}$). But then $\\frac1p=\\frac{23}{30}-\\frac12=\\frac4{15}$ and $p=\\frac{15}4$, not an integer. One term is impossible because $\\frac{23}{30}$ is not a unit fraction. So the minimum is $n=3$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 3**"
      }
